A fair six-sided die has the numbers 1 through 6. The composite numbers are 4 and 6, the prime numbers are 2, 3, and 5, and the number 1 requires a re-roll. Therefore, the probability of rolling a composite number (4 or 6) on a single roll is:
\[
P(\text{composite}) = \frac{2}{6} = \frac{1}{3}
\]
The probability of rolling a prime number (2, 3, or 5) on a single roll is:
\[
P(\text{prime}) = \frac{3}{6} = \frac{1}{2}
\]
The probability of rolling a 1 on a single roll is:
\[
P(1) = \frac{1}{6}
\]
Since rolling a 1 requires a re-roll, the effective probability of rolling a composite number or a prime number on a single roll, considering the re-roll, is:
\[
P(\text{composite or prime}) = \frac{5}{6}
\]
The probability of rolling a composite number on a successful roll (i.e., after excluding the re-roll) is:
\[
P(\text{composite} \mid \text{composite or prime}) = \frac{2}{5}
\]
The probability of rolling a prime number on a successful roll is:
\[
P(\text{prime} \mid \text{composite or prime}) = \frac{3}{5}
\]
