\]

Next, we consider the hexagons surrounding the outermost layer of hexagons. Each of these hexagons is adjacent to two hexagons from the outermost layer and one hexagon from the second outermost layer. Since the hexagons in the outermost layer are either yellow or green, and the hexagons in the second outermost layer are either yellow or green but not the same color as their adjacent hexagons in the outermost layer, each hexagon in the third outermost layer has 2 choices of color (either yellow or green) as long as it is not the same color as its adjacent hexagons. However, since the pattern of coloring the outermost layer is already fixed, the coloring of the third outermost layer is also fixed by the same logic.

